ITA-MC-2018-SP-ING-Q006	2018	single_phase	ingles	6	Marque a opção correta de acordo com o sentido com que os verbos modais sublinhados são empregados no texto. I. Can living with less make you happier? (título) → para indicar uma possibilidade. II. We never know what tomorrow might bring, (linha 13) → para indicar um estado contrário à realidade. III. It may sound as if I’m exaggerating… (linha 35) → para indicar uma probabilidade. IV. I think you should try saying goodbye to some of your things. (linha 39) → para dar um conselho. Estão corretas	A: I e II. || B: I, II e IV. || C: I, III e IV. || D: II, III e IV. || E: II e IV. As questões de 7 a 12 referem-se ao texto a seguir: WE RECORDED VCs CONVERSATIONS AND ANALYZED HOW DIFFERENTLY THEY TALK ABOUT FEMALE ENTREPRENEURS When venture capitalists (VCs) evaluate investment proposals, the language they use to describe the 1 entrepreneurs who write them plays an important but often hidden role in shaping who is awarded funding 2 and why.[…] We were given access to government venture capital decision making meetings in Sweden and 3 were able to observe the types of language that VCs used over a two-year period. One major thing stuck out: 4 The language used to describe male and female entrepreneurs was radically different. And these differences 5 have very real consequences for those seeking funding — and for society in general. 6 […] Worldwide, government venture capital is important for bridging significant financial gaps and 7 supporting innovation and growth, as VCs can take risks where banks are not allowed to. When uncertainty is 8 high regarding assessment of product and market potential, for example, the assessment of the 9 entrepreneur’s potential becomes highly central in government VCs’ decision making. 10 In Sweden, about one-third of businesses are owned and run by women, although they are not granted 11 a corresponding proportion of government funding. In fact, women-owned businesses receive much less — 12 only 13%–18%, the rest going to male-owned companies. 13 This brings us back to our research. From 2009 to 2010 we were invited to silently observe 14 governmental VC decision-making meetings and, more important, the conversations they had about 15 entrepreneurs applying for funding. […] We observed closed-room, face-to-face discussions leading final 16 funding decisions for 125 venture applications. Of these, 99 (79%) were from male entrepreneurs and 26 17 (21%) were from female entrepreneurs. The group of government venture capitalists observed included 18 seven individuals: two women and five men. […] 19 Aside from a few exceptions, the financiers rhetorically produce stereotypical images of women as 20 having qualities opposite to those considered important to being an entrepreneur, with VCs questioning their 21 credibility, trustworthiness, experience, and knowledge. 22 Conversely, when assessing male entrepreneurs, financiers leaned on stereotypical beliefs about men 23 that reinforced their entrepreneurial potential. Male entrepreneurs were commonly described as being 24 assertive, innovative, competent, experienced, knowledgeable, and having established networks. 25 We developed male and female entrepreneur personas based on our findings […]. These personas 26 highlight a few key differences in how the entrepreneurs were perceived depending on their gender. Men 27 were characterized as having entrepreneurial potential, while the entrepreneurial potential for women was 28 diminished. Many of the young men and women were described as being young, though youth for men was 29 viewed as promising, while young women were considered inexperienced. Men were praised for being 30 viewed as aggressive or arrogant, while women’s experience and excitement were tempered by discussions 31 of their emotional shortcomings. Similarly, cautiousness was viewed very differently depending on the gender 32 of the entrepreneur. 33 Unsurprisingly, these stereotypes seem to have played a role in who got funding and who didn’t. 34 Women entrepreneurs were only awarded, on average, 25% of the applied-for amount, whereas men 35 received, on average, 52% of what they asked for. Women were also denied financing to a greater extent 36 than men, with close to 53% of women having their applications dismissed, compared with 38% of men. […] 37 Such stereotyping will inevitably influence the distribution of financing, but could also have other major 38 consequences. Because the purpose of government venture capital is to use tax money to stimulate growth 39 and value creation for society as a whole, gender bias presents the risk that the money isn’t being invested in 40 businesses that have the highest potential. This isn’t only damaging for women entrepreneurs; it’s potentially 41 damaging for society as a whole. 42 Fonte: Adaptado de Harvard Business Review <https://hbr.org/2017/05/we-recorded-vcs-conversations-and-analyzed- how-differently-they-talk-about-female-entrepreneurs>. Acesso em: 17 mai. 2017
